<BaseLayout title="Bunio — Coffee Platform | technical case study" description="Bunio (Coffee Platform): a multi-tenant loyalty platform for coffee shops with safe purchase accounting and guest identification." lang="en" alternate="/projects/coffee-platform/">
  <article class="container case">
    <a class="back" href="/en/#projects">← Home</a>
    <header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">ENGINEERING CASE · PERSONAL PROJECT</p><h1>Bunio</h1><p class="lead">Coffee Platform is a multi-tenant platform for coffee shops, combining guest loyalty, staff tools, and organization management.</p><p class="status">In development · production deployment and pilot are not confirmed</p><p class="stack">PHP 8.3+ · Laravel 13 · REST API · Vue 3 · TypeScript · PostgreSQL 17 · Redis 7 · Docker</p></header>

    <section><h2>Problem and domain</h2><p>An independent coffee shop needs a clear way to account for paid purchases and rewards, a guest card, staff workspace, and tools to manage its catalog. Bunio brings these flows together while keeping each organization’s data and rules in its own business context.</p><p>Loyalty is more than a number on a profile. A purchase must belong to a selected coffee shop, an active staff member, a guest account, and a verified identification. A retry after a network failure must not turn one purchase into two.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Architecture</h2><p>The backend is a Laravel modular monolith with a REST API; the frontend is a separate Vue 3 SPA. Sanctum cookie sessions and CSRF protect API interactions. PostgreSQL stores organizations, memberships, accounts, and the operation ledger; Redis/Lua handles short-lived QR and numeric codes.</p><p>Docker Compose brings together PHP-FPM, Nginx, PostgreSQL, Redis, Node/Vite, and Mailpit for local development. A Blade panel for platform Super Admin is separate from the Vue interfaces for guests, staff, and organization admins.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Multi-tenancy: one database, scoped contexts</h2><p>Organizations share PostgreSQL and the same schema. Isolation is logical: each business record is associated with an organization, the API loads it from the route, and the backend checks related-record ownership, membership, and permissions. A guest has a separate LoyaltyAccount in each organization; staff memberships and roles do not carry across organizations.</p><p>A shared database simplifies relational integrity, migrations, and operating a single MVP. The trade-off is that isolation depends on correctly scoping queries and authorization throughout the backend: a missing organization filter can expose or alter another tenant’s data. The backend therefore validates tenant context instead of trusting a slug supplied by the interface.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Roles: platform and organization are separate</h2><p>A guest uses their own account and guest features. Active staff accrue paid purchases and redeem rewards; an organization admin also manages memberships, catalog, and news within that organization.</p><p>Super Admin is a separate platform role for the platform panel and support tools. That status alone does not grant access to an organization’s loyalty operations. To accrue or redeem, a Super Admin must also have an active staff/admin membership in that organization. Global administration does not silently become business-level access.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Loyalty as an operation ledger</h2><p>Each user has a separate account for each organization. The account stores cumulative `total_purchased`, `total_rewards_earned`, and `total_rewards_redeemed`; transactions explain each change and are not edited by ordinary business operations. The current rule is one reward for every nine paid drinks. Redemption reduces available rewards but does not erase purchase history.</p><p>A batch accrual can cross several thresholds: newly earned rewards are calculated from the difference between threshold counts before and after the purchase. Account counters and the ledger entry change in one transaction. Rewards can be saved for later; selecting a specific drink and corrective operations are not implemented yet.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Guest identification</h2><p>A guest presents a dynamic pair: a QR containing a cryptographically random token and a separate six-digit code. Both are scoped to an organization, live for 60 seconds, and the QR contains no personal data. Reissuing uses a request ID; Redis/Lua atomically updates the pair, indexes, and TTL, while the issue result is retained for 300 seconds to support safe retries.</p><p>Staff resolves either the QR or code in the organization context. A successful resolve atomically consumes the whole pair, so the other identifier cannot be presented to obtain another proof. The backend issues a one-time identification proof valid for 120 seconds and bound to the organization, account, and staff member; PostgreSQL stores only its hash. A new operation requires a new identification.</p></section>

    <section class="deep"><p class="eyebrow">DEEPER ENGINEERING DIVE</p><h2>Preventing duplicate accrual after retries and concurrent requests</h2><p>A staff member submits a purchase accrual. The request reaches the server, but its response is lost or arrives after a timeout. The client cannot tell whether the operation committed. Creating a new UUID is dangerous: it represents a new operation and could accrue the purchase twice. The safe retry reuses the same organization, UUID, request body, and original proof.</p>
      <figure class="flow"><figcaption>Retry after an unknown outcome</figcaption><p class="flow-hint">The sequence scrolls horizontally to show the full flow.</p><div class="flow-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Purchase accrual sequence after a timeout"><svg viewBox="0 0 1000 570" role="img" aria-labelledby="flow-title flow-desc" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="flow-title">Idempotent purchase accrual after a timeout</title><desc id="flow-desc">First request: Staff sends UUID, parameters, and proof. In a transaction, the API locks organization, staff membership, and account, then checks idempotency; for a new operation it validates and locks proof, updates counters, writes the ledger, consumes proof, and commits. It emits the event after commit. The response is lost to a timeout. A retry with the same request rechecks organization, membership, and account, finds the prior transaction, compares parameters and linked consumed proof, and returns the same record without another accrual event.</desc><defs><marker id="m-arrow" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="7" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L8 4L0 8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></marker></defs><g class="actor"><rect x="20" y="25" width="185" height="58" rx="8"/><text x="112" y="50" class="ttl" text-anchor="middle">Staff</text><text x="112" y="69" class="sub" text-anchor="middle">client</text></g><g class="actor"><rect x="285" y="25" width="200" height="58" rx="8"/><text x="385" y="50" class="ttl" text-anchor="middle">Laravel API</text><text x="385" y="69" class="sub" text-anchor="middle">Action</text></g><g class="actor"><rect x="595" y="25" width="190" height="58" rx="8"/><text x="690" y="50" class="ttl" text-anchor="middle">PostgreSQL</text><text x="690" y="69" class="sub" text-anchor="middle">transaction</text></g><g class="actor"><rect x="835" y="25" width="145" height="58" rx="8"/><text x="907" y="50" class="ttl" text-anchor="middle">Event</text><text x="907" y="69" class="sub" text-anchor="middle">after commit</text></g><path class="life" d="M112 83V565M385 83V565M690 83V565M907 83V565"/><path class="msg" d="M205 125H277" marker-end="url(#m-arrow)"/><text x="241" y="115" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">1 · request</text><text x="215" y="145" class="sub">UUID + body + proof</text><path class="msg" d="M485 185H587" marker-end="url(#m-arrow)"/><text x="536" y="175" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">2 · BEGIN + locks</text><text x="600" y="205" class="sub">organization → membership → account</text><path class="msg" d="M485 240H587" marker-end="url(#m-arrow)"/><text x="536" y="230" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">3 · check UUID in account</text><text x="600" y="260" class="sub">no record: proof → counters → ledger</text><path class="msg" d="M485 290H587" marker-end="url(#m-arrow)"/><text x="536" y="280" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">4 · COMMIT</text><path class="msg" d="M587 315H485" marker-end="url(#m-arrow)"/><text x="536" y="305" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">commit confirmed</text><path class="msg" d="M485 340H895" marker-end="url(#m-arrow)"/><text x="690" y="330" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">5 · account update after commit</text><path class="msg timeout" d="M277 375H205" marker-end="url(#m-arrow)"/><text x="241" y="365" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">response lost: timeout</text><path class="msg retry" d="M205 425H277" marker-end="url(#m-arrow)"/><text x="241" y="415" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">6 · same request</text><text x="215" y="445" class="sub">same UUID, body, proof</text><path class="msg" d="M485 475H587" marker-end="url(#m-arrow)"/><text x="536" y="465" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">7 · find and verify record</text><text x="600" y="495" class="sub">no new accrual or proof lock</text><path class="msg" d="M587 525H485" marker-end="url(#m-arrow)"/><text x="536" y="515" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">same transaction</text><path class="msg" d="M285 555H205" marker-end="url(#m-arrow)"/><text x="245" y="545" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">8 · return result</text></svg></div><p class="flow-note">Reusing the key with a different staff member, quantity, operation type, or proof returns a conflict. No new accrual is created.</p></figure>
      <p>The lock order in the implementation matters: first the organization row, then the staff membership, then the account, and finally the proof. The organization must be active; membership must be active and have the staff/admin role; the account is loaded within that organization. Only then is the UUID checked against the ledger for that account.</p><p>If a record exists, the backend compares operation type, staff member, quantity, and the hash of the original proof linked to that transaction and already consumed. If they match, it returns the existing record. Different parameters raise an idempotency conflict; no new entry is written.</p><p>For a new operation, proof is validated and locked. A single transaction updates counters, inserts the ledger entry, and marks the proof consumed. The account update event is emitted through `afterCommit`, so clients are not notified about a change that could still roll back.</p><p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> locking the organization first creates one consistent order and protects checks of its active state and membership from races with access changes. It also serializes loyalty operations across the whole organization, even when requests touch different accounts, which limits parallelism. Account locks additionally serialize changes to a particular balance, while the proof lock prevents concurrent reuse of one confirmation.</p>
    </section>

    <section><h2>Testing, security, and quality</h2><p>Checks cover retries with the same UUID, key reuse with different parameters, proof consumption and reuse, expired identification, and insufficient balance. PostgreSQL integration tests exercise concurrent accrual and locks; Redis/Lua tests cover atomic pair consumption, collisions, reissue, and TTL.</p><p>The backend checks organization, active membership, and role independently of the frontend. Sanctum/CSRF protect the SPA API; identifiers are hashed or short-lived, and personal data is not embedded in QR codes. The operation ledger is kept separately from current counters. Automatic corrections and selecting a reward drink are not implemented yet.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Implemented and next</h2><div class="status-grid"><article><h3>Implemented</h3><ul><li>Separate accounts and roles per organization.</li><li>Guest QR/code and one-time proof.</li><li>Accrual, reward redemption, ledger, and idempotency.</li><li>Catalog, news, guest/staff/admin interfaces, and a separate Super Admin panel.</li><li>Transaction history and verified realtime loyalty updates across devices.</li></ul></article><article><h3>Next steps</h3><ul><li>Multiple branches and staff assignment to branches.</li><li>Corrective operations and choosing a drink at redemption.</li><li>Camera QR scanning, Web Push, and a complete PWA.</li><li>Production deployment and pilot are not confirmed.</li></ul></article></div><p class="note">The repository is private. Source code and internal data are not published; I’m happy to discuss architecture and selected implementation decisions in a technical interview.</p></section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
